-- title:
--   Macaulay's unmixedness for $K[X_\sigma]$, $K$ algebraically closed and $\sigma$ finite: every associated prime of $K[X_\sigma]/(rs)$, $rs$ weakly regular, has height at most $|rs|$
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and $\sigma$ a finite type. Then `UnmixedHeight (MvPolynomial σ K)` holds: for every weakly regular sequence $r_1, \dots, r_k$ of the polynomial ring $K[X_\sigma]$, every associated prime of $K[X_\sigma]/(r_1, \dots, r_k)$ has height at most $k$. This is the inequality half of Macaulay's unmixedness theorem for $K[X_\sigma]$; the reverse inequality is not part of the statement. A sequence is weakly regular when each entry is a nonzerodivisor modulo the ideal generated by the earlier ones (Mathlib's `IsWeaklyRegular`), and heights are taken in $\mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}$. The name `UnmixedHeight` is that of the published Definition `DiazHilbertBox` (module `Definitions.Def_DiazHilbertBox`). It is the hypothesis `hU : UnmixedHeight _` of round C1.1 of the Diaz modulus mission (in `DiazModulus.exists_isWeaklyRegular_append_of_height`, `DiazModulus.boxHilb_le_of_height`, `DiazModulus.boxHilb_hilbert_side` and `DiazModulus.torus_hilbert_side`). For `DiazModulus.torus_hilbert_side` the ring is the polynomial ring $K[X, Y, Z]$, so for $K$ algebraically closed, for instance $K = \mathbb{C}$, this node supplies that hypothesis.
--
--   **Proof.** Let $S = K[X_\sigma]$ and let $\mathfrak{p}$ be an associated prime of $S/(rs)$; it contains $rs$. Choose a maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m} \supseteq \mathfrak{p}$ and let $A = S_\mathfrak{m}$, a Noetherian local ring. By the Nullstellensatz $\mathfrak{m}$ is the ideal of a point $a$, so `DiazModulus.exists_isWeaklyRegular_vanishing` gives a weakly regular sequence of $|\sigma|$ elements of $\mathfrak{m}$; localised, it is a regular sequence in the maximal ideal of $A$, and $\dim A = \operatorname{ht} \mathfrak{m} \le \dim S = |\sigma|$. The sequence $rs$ localises in the same way to a regular sequence in the maximal ideal of $A$, and $\mathfrak{p}A$ is an associated prime of $A/(rs)A$ with the height of $\mathfrak{p}$. `DiazModulus.height_le_length_of_mem_associatedPrimes` then gives $\operatorname{ht} \mathfrak{p} = \operatorname{ht} \mathfrak{p}A \le |rs|$.
--
--   **Novelty.** Classical; routine formalisation. Only the inequality half is stated, and only over an algebraically closed field.
-- source:
--   Macaulay's unmixedness theorem (F. S. Macaulay, The Algebraic Theory of Modular Systems, Cambridge University Press, 1916) for polynomial rings over a field, which are Cohen–Macaulay; in this form W. Bruns and J. Herzog, Cohen–Macaulay Rings, Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 39, Cambridge University Press, Th. 2.1.6, and H. Matsumura, Commutative Ring Theory, Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 8, Cambridge University Press, §17. Round C1.1 of the Diaz modulus mission carries it as the hypothesis UnmixedHeight, in place of Macaulay's theorem as quoted in M. Waldschmidt, Diophantine Approximation on Linear Algebraic Groups, Grundlehren der mathematischen Wissenschaften 326, Springer, 2000, §8.2.1 (p. 235; Chapter 8 is by D. Roy). Uses DiazModulus.height_le_length_of_mem_associatedPrimes and DiazModulus.exists_isWeaklyRegular_vanishing. C1.2 of the Diaz modulus mission. Formal proof: Diaz modulus mission, 29 October 2026 (C. Perassi).

import Definitions.Def_DiazHilbertBox

